---
// src/components/blog/visuals/TantrumVsMeltdownVisual.astro
---

<div class="apple-glass-card apple-specular-top my-10 p-5 sm:p-7 rounded-3xl border border-gray-100 shadow-sm bg-gradient-to-br from-white/95 via-gray-50/50 to-amber-50/20 text-left">
  <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 mb-4">
    <span class="w-2.5 h-2.5 rounded-full bg-brand-gold"></span>
    <h3 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-lg sm:text-xl tracking-tight m-0">
      Clinical Comparison: Neurological Meltdown vs. Behavioral Tantrum
    </h3>
  </div>
  
  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-600 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    Parents and educators often confuse meltdowns with tantrums. Neurologically, they originate from entirely different regions of the brain and require opposing clinical interventions.
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:gap-6">
    <!-- Tantrum Column -->
    <div class="bg-blue-50/70 border border-blue-200/80 rounded-2xl p-4 sm:p-5 flex flex-col">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <span class="text-xs font-extrabold uppercase tracking-wider text-blue-900 bg-blue-100 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">
          Goal-Driven &bull; Tantrum
        </span>
        <span class="text-xs text-blue-700 font-semibold">Prefrontal Cortex Active</span>
      </div>

      <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-base mb-2">Behavioral Expression</h4>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        The child is frustrated because they want something specific (a toy, more screen time, dessert). Their cognitive control is partially intact.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2 text-xs text-gray-700 mt-auto pt-3 border-t border-blue-200/60">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-blue-700 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span><strong>Audience Aware:</strong> Checks adult facial reactions; stops if ignored.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-blue-700 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span><strong>Safety Intact:</strong> Rarely causes self-harm; aware of physical space.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-blue-700 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span><strong>Clinical Strategy:</strong> Hold calm, firm loving boundaries; do not reward screams.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Meltdown Column -->
    <div class="bg-amber-50/70 border border-amber-300/80 rounded-2xl p-4 sm:p-5 flex flex-col">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
        <span class="text-xs font-extrabold uppercase tracking-wider text-amber-950 bg-amber-200 px-2.5 py-1 rounded-full">
          Neurological &bull; Meltdown
        </span>
        <span class="text-xs text-amber-800 font-semibold">Amygdala Hijack</span>
      </div>

      <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-navy text-base mb-2">Autonomic Overwhelm</h4>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        A fight, flight, or freeze response caused by sensory flooding, cognitive fatigue, or anxiety. The executive brain is completely offline.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-2 text-xs text-gray-700 mt-auto pt-3 border-t border-amber-300/60">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-amber-800 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span><strong>No Audience Needed:</strong> Continues even in an empty room; child cannot stop.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-amber-800 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span><strong>Safety at Risk:</strong> Trembling, hitting, thrashing, genuine panic.</span>
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-start gap-1.5">
          <span class="text-amber-800 font-bold">&bull;</span>
          <span><strong>Clinical Strategy:</strong> Reduce sensory load, eliminate talking, co-regulate safely.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-4 p-3 bg-white/80 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 text-xs text-gray-600 italic text-center">
    💡 <strong>Clinical Takeaway:</strong> You cannot discipline a child out of a neurological meltdown. You must lower demands and restore nervous system safety before any conversation can occur.
  </div>
</div>
